#
# Consumed as `verible-verilog-lint --waiver_files=...` by tests/run-style.sh.
#
# This file is for findings that are correct about the code but wrong about what the
# code should be. A rule that is wrong *everywhere* belongs in .rules.verible_lint
# instead, disabled once with its reason; this file is for the narrow exceptions.
#
# Two rules for editing it, the same two verilator-waivers.vlt sets:
#
#   1. Every waiver carries its reason. A waiver with no reason is indistinguishable
#      from a bug someone hid.
#   2. Every waiver is scoped as narrowly as the syntax allows. --regex matches the
#      text of the offending line, so it can name the exact identifiers being excused;
#      a bare --location would waive the whole rule for the whole file and hide the
#      next occurrence in code nobody has looked at.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The DE0-Nano board's own port names
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CLOCK_50, GPIO0, GPIO1 and LED are signals in the design that are not free to be
# renamed. DomesdayDuplicator.qsf binds them by name across 164 lines of
# pin-location and I/O-standard assignment, and the .SDC creates the 50 MHz clock
# with `[get_ports CLOCK_50]`. Renaming them means editing every one of those lines
# in step, and a single typo yields a board that programs successfully and drives the
# wrong pin — a failure that looks like broken hardware, not like a typo.
#
# They are also not this project's names to choose: they are Terasic's, printed in
# the DE0-Nano documentation that anyone wiring the board reads alongside this
# source.
#
# Scoped to the four names and to the top level, so a new ALL_CAPS signal anywhere —
# including a new port on this same module — is still reported.
waive --rule=signal-name-style --regex="\b(CLOCK_50|GPIO0|GPIO1|LED)\b" --location=".*DomesdayDuplicator\.v"
waive --rule=signal-name-style --regex="\b(CLOCK_50|GPIO0|GPIO1|LED)\b" --location=".*DomesdayDuplicatorFactory\.v"

# The audio converters' ports, which are the same case. ADC_CS_N, ADC_SADDR, ADC_SCLK
# and ADC_SDAT are the DE0-Nano's on-board ADC128S022, and GPIO_2 and GPIO_2_IN its
# 2x13 header JP3, named exactly as Terasic's manual and reference .qsf name them and
# bound by those names in DomesdayDuplicator.qsf for the same reasons as the four above.
# Only the application image has them, so only its top level is waived.
waive --rule=signal-name-style --regex="\b(ADC_CS_N|ADC_SADDR|ADC_SCLK|ADC_SDAT|GPIO_2|GPIO_2_IN)\b" --location=".*DomesdayDuplicator\.v"

# The capture gateware's top level also has the DE0-Nano's SDRAM pins, under Terasic's
# names for them - DRAM_ADDR, DRAM_BA, DRAM_CAS_N, DRAM_CKE, DRAM_CLK, DRAM_CS_N,
# DRAM_DQ, DRAM_DQM, DRAM_RAS_N and DRAM_WE_N - for the same two reasons: the .qsf
# binds them by name, 39 pins of location and I/O standard plus the I/O cell register
# assignments, and they are the names in the board's manual and reference project.
# Scoped to those ten names and to that one top level; the factory image has no SDRAM.
waive --rule=signal-name-style --regex="\bDRAM_(ADDR|BA|CAS_N|CKE|CLK|CS_N|DQ|DQM|RAS_N|WE_N)\b" --location=".*DomesdayDuplicator\.v"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The megafunction's own parameter names
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# common/sim/altremote_update.v is a model of an Altera megafunction, and its
# parameter list is that megafunction's: operation_mode, in_data_width,
# config_device_addr_width and the rest are lower case because Altera named them
# that way, and Quartus resolves them by name when the real one is instantiated.
#
# Renaming them to this project's UpperCamelCase would make the model disagree
# with the part it stands in for, which is the one thing a model may never do —
# a design that simulated against it would fail to synthesise, and the failure
# would name a parameter that exists in neither file.
#
# Scoped to that one file. A parameter anywhere else, including elsewhere in
# common/sim, is still held to the convention.
waive --rule=parameter-name-style --location=".*sim/altremote_update\.v"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The application PLL's parameter block
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# application/applicationPll.v instantiates altpll and sets its parameters with
# defparam, which the lowRISC guide forbids in favour of #( ) overrides. The file is
# written in the MegaWizard's idiom on purpose, so that it reads side by side with
# common/IPpllGenerator.v - the same parameters in the same form - and a change to one
# PLL can be checked against the other line by line. That is worth more here than the
# rule, which exists to stop parameters being set from a distance in a design's own
# hierarchy; this is one megafunction's parameters set beside its one instantiation.
#
# Scoped to that one file. A defparam anywhere else is still reported.
waive --rule=forbid-defparam --location=".*application/applicationPll\.v"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The application PLL's black box
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# application/applicationPll_bb.v declares module applicationPll, which is what a
# black box is: the same module name as the file it stands in for, so the top level's
# instantiation resolves to it when linting and to applicationPll.v when compiling.
# Naming the module after this file would make it stand in for nothing. The
# correctness linter's equivalent finding is waived for the same reason in
# verilator-waivers.vlt.
waive --rule=module-filename --location=".*application/applicationPll_bb\.v"
